title: Log storage
tags: [[Event log]]
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: What an operation records, its before and after snapshot rows, why versions are recomputed, and how one write lays it all down.

The log lives in the repository's store, beside the data it describes — one transaction
writes both ([[Store tables]]): a ''revision'' holds its timestamp, label and origin; an
''operation'' the fields below; each operation its before and after ''snapshot'' rows;
HEAD — the newest operation, the change feed's cursor — is a record of its own. (Watcher
events are not here: they are buffered in memory.) The log is a sequence: operations are
numbered in the order they were written, and none names a parent ([[The operation log]],
[[Why the log stops being a tree]]). The names below — `operation`, `op_snapshot`,
`log_head` — are those of the SQLite tables of before September 2026.

|!Operation field |!Holds |
|`id` |never reused; its order is the log's |
|`rev_id`, `seq` |its revision, and its place in it (from 1) |
|`op_type` |[[Revisions and operations]] |
|`entity_uuid` |the metarecord it changed |
|`entity_version_before`, `…_after` |the metarecord's version on each side — provenance, not authority |
|`field_name` |for a field operation |
|`reverts_op_id` |for an operation a revert wrote: the one it undid |

The store also keeps each revision's operations in order and each metarecord's
operations, so a revision's listing and a metarecord's history are each a prefix read; the
newest operations are a backward range.

`reverts_op_id` is read by displays and by the undo selection — which is why a revert
records it rather than merely looking like one. Nothing on the write path branches on it,
and it may dangle: pruning and retention may remove the operation it names.

!! Snapshots

Each operation stores its state before and after as rows in the column form of a field
row ([[The column form of a value]]), each keeping its original field id:

|!Type |!Before |!After |
|`create_metarecord` |— |every row of the new metarecord |
|`delete_metarecord` |every row of the deleted one |— |
|`set_field` |the previous rows of the name |the new rows |
|`append_field` |— |the appended row |
|`delete_field` |the deleted rows |— |
|`file_deleted` |the `mfr_path` row |one `nothing` row |
|`file_moved` |the previous `mfr_path` row |the new one |
|`file_modified` |`mfr_size`, `mfr_mtime` and the hash rows |the new `mfr_size`, `mfr_mtime` |

A directory's `file_deleted` gives each descendant its own operation in the same revision.

!! Versions recomputed

A row id is never reused, and a row a revert puts back gets a fresh one ([[What a revert writes]]). The version is never restored from the log either: it is a hash of the content
([[Metarecord version]]), so the state a revert writes has the version it should.
The `entity_version_*` fields stay, costing nothing at write time, for clients correlating
an operation with something observed outside; nothing reads them to decide what to write.

!! One write

Every request that changes metadata goes through one `log::Writer`, in one transaction:
a revision is opened (with the request's label); for each change, the affected rows and
the version are read (the before snapshot), the change is applied (the version updated
incrementally), the resulting rows read (the after snapshot), and an operation appended;
finally HEAD is recorded and the transaction commits. Every writer — reconcile included — follows it, which is what
keeps the data and HEAD consistent ([[Writes and atomicity]]).

(An `unknown` operation type exists for an unlogged write — no snapshots, so nothing can
revert it. No endpoint writes one: the `no_log` option it was meant for was never needed.)
